      "problem": "The slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(1).$ In part 1, we found\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{-2({x}^{2}+2x-8)}{(2{x}^{2}-11x+5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{-2({1}^{2}+2\\cdot 1-8)}{(2\\cdot {1}^{2}-11\\cdot 1+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=1$ is $\\frac{5}{8}.$",
